          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been on a proton pump inhibitor for six years. Is long-term use a concern, what does guidance describe about review, and how is reflux coded?",
          "answer": "**Safety first:** Seek urgent medical care straight away if you have trouble or pain when swallowing, vomit blood, pass black stools, or have unexplained weight loss. Chest pain should never be assumed to be reflux. The guideline says cardiac causes come first, especially if the pain comes with breathlessness, sweating, or exertion (GERD guideline, per the research summary). Do not stop or change your PPI on your own.\n\n**Long-term use and risks:** The documents searched did not contain anything on the risks of long-term PPI use, such as effects on bones, kidneys, or vitamin levels. So neither the strength of any evidence nor any disagreement about risks can be reported here.\n\n**Review guidance:** The two sources disagree. The research summary says the GERD guideline (empiric initial management and alarm features) advises that long-term acid suppression, where genuinely needed, be continued at the lowest effective intensity with periodic review, and not renewed indefinitely without reassessment. The consultation summary says the only guidance it found covers first-step management, including an initial trial of acid-reducing treatment taken before a meal, and does not cover long-term use or review intervals. Both agree the guideline does not say how often review should happen. Your prescriber can tell you which applies to your situation.\n\n**Coding:** Reflux is coded as K21 in ICD-10-CM, the digestive diseases chapter. The main split is whether esophagitis is present, which needs an endoscopy finding; most records fall under the without-esophagitis branch. On the code for long-term drug use, the sources differ in coverage. The research summary says long-term drug therapy is recorded under a separate \"factors influencing health\" category, but it did not give the rules for choosing between codes. The consultation summary could not retrieve any long-term medication code. Your clinician or medical coder can confirm which codes appear in your records.\n\n**Next step:** Six years on one medicine is a good reason to ask your prescriber whether it is still needed and whether any monitoring is advisable.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
